//! The slab budget: how many of each object this kernel can hold, the stride
//! reserved per thread context, and the sentinel each table answers with when
//! it has no slot to give.
//!
//! These are one decision. Every number is a fixed-size table's dimension and
//! they are chosen against each other (`MAX_ATTACHMENTS` is the sum of the
//! waitables, because an attachment is one (Waiter, waitable) pair and spec
//! §2.2 allows an object at most one Waiter). A derived `static` built from an
//! imported const is not a compile-time length, so a derived size and its
//! operands cannot be split across modules; `MAX_ATTACHMENTS` has to sit beside
//! the terms it adds.
//!
//! No slab scan or handle walk in the kernel takes a preemption point. Every
//! free-slot search, by-line or by-key lookup and teardown sweep is bounded by
//! one of the constants below, or by the `capacity()` of a slab that donation
//! has grown, over an O(1) body in RAM, so there is no masked window for a
//! point to shorten. Raising a bound by an order of magnitude is what would
//! make that worth re-checking.

import hal
import sosabi.{ClockType}

/// Process bodies the kernel can hold, compiled floor: root plus the two
/// children a driver-as-service launch needs (a driver child that owns the UART
/// and a client child that reads it through a pipe).
///
/// A body is what a live process runs in (spec §2.7). The teardown gives it
/// back, so this bounds the processes alive at once; the dead ones somebody
/// still holds are records, bounded by `MAX_PROCESS_RECORDS`.
///
/// Raising this number is the only edit needed. Every per-process table is
/// indexed rather than assumed: the handle table is a field of `ProcessBody`,
/// and the thread, event, waiter, interrupt and timer slabs carry a `process`
/// field their teardown scans by.
///
/// A body is not free. Each one costs:
///
///   - `MAX_HANDLES` handle entries in `ProcessBody.handles` and as many in
///     `ProcessBody.boot_handles`;
///   - one `MAX_GRANT_ROWS`-row grant record and one `ProcessBody`;
///   - `PROCESS_RECORDS_PER_BODY` records, which `MAX_PROCESS_RECORDS` derives
///     from it.
///
/// It does not cost protection rows. Those are per domain and reprogrammed at
/// `load_domain`, so `hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET` is what one process may hold
/// installed, and another process adds nothing to it. Root spends
/// `IMAGE_GRANT_ROWS` (its two loadable segments plus the kernel's stack grant)
/// whatever a launch wires up, because a launcher hands out capabilities and
/// maps nothing: `process_create` reads a child's image with kernel privilege
/// and a driver child maps its own window.
///
/// This is the smallest floor that serves the launch shape above; a larger one
/// is an edit to this line when something needs it.
///
/// This is the compiled floor. `SlabKind.Processes` raises the machine's real
/// ceiling, and a body donation carries the handle table, the boot set and the
/// grant record with it, since they are fields of `ProcessBody`.
///
/// On a tier that translates addresses there is a second, lower ceiling: a
/// persistent per-process table set comes out of a static HAL pool that
/// donation cannot reach, so `hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` is what a create actually
/// meets there. See the assert below and `kcore.process.process_slots_usable`.
public(package) static MAX_PROCESSES: Int = 3

/// How many process records the kernel holds per body, compiled floor.
///
/// A body is spent again as soon as its process dies, but the record stays for
/// as long as a handle or an attachment names it, and every live process holds
/// one too. So the record table is the larger, and the factor is what a
/// supervisor that keeps its dead children's handles can hold: one less than it
/// per body before a create is refused. It must be greater than one, and it is
/// generous because a record is small.
public(package) static PROCESS_RECORDS_PER_BODY: Int = 4

static_assert(PROCESS_RECORDS_PER_BODY > 1,
              "the record table must hold dead processes beside the live ones")

/// Process records the kernel can hold, compiled floor (spec §2.7).
///
/// A record is the process as handles, attachments and charged objects name
/// it: its state, status word, quota ledger, reference count, attachment, final
/// trap counters and the link to its body. `SlabKind.ProcessRecords` raises
/// the machine's real ceiling, separately from the bodies: demand for records
/// follows how long holders keep dead processes, not how many run.
///
/// Each one also costs a term in `MAX_ATTACHMENTS`, because a Process handle is
/// a waitable and an attachment on one names the record.
public(package) static MAX_PROCESS_RECORDS: Int = PROCESS_RECORDS_PER_BODY * MAX_PROCESSES

/// Root's record. The kernel loads exactly one image (spec §12) and its
/// process takes the first record and the first body (`ROOT_BODY`), so
/// "process 0" and "root" are the same statement; that is what lets
/// `end_process` branch on a comparison rather than a flag.
public(package) static ROOT_PROCESS: Int = 0

/// Root's body, the first of the body table as root's record is the first of
/// the record table.
public(package) static ROOT_BODY: Int = 0

/// "No process". The plain-form sentinel for a record index, like `NO_THREAD`.
public(package) static NO_PROCESS: Int = -1

/// "No body": a record whose process is dead, or not yet created, names none.
public(package) static NO_BODY: Int = -1

/// Memory objects the kernel can hold (spec §2.5).
///
/// This is a real allocation budget: `MemoryOp.Split` creates one per cut, so
/// the slab is what a pool is spent through. The number is the boot rows plus
/// room for a handful of derived regions, which is what a hand-written system's
/// launcher needs. A dropped region returns its slot on last release and its
/// range goes back onto its pool root's free list, so this number bounds
/// concurrent regions rather than total cuts (`tests/memory-split` runs more
/// split rounds than there are slots with nothing refused). The workload is
/// bounded by concurrency in both currencies: regions alive at once, and bytes
/// out at once.
public(package) static MAX_MEMORIES: Int = 16

/// The same sentinel for the Memory slab.
public(package) static NO_MEMORY: Int = -1

/// Free-range nodes a pool's free list can hold, over all pools.
///
/// A node is only ever needed for a hole. A range returned adjacent to another
/// free range coalesces into it, and one adjacent to the pool's unspent
/// frontier folds back into the cursor and needs no node. So the list is as
/// long as the pool is fragmented, and this number is generous against
/// `MAX_MEMORIES`: every live region could be released out of order, each
/// landing as its own hole, and the list would still have nodes to spare.
///
/// Exhaustion must not fault a release. A range that finds no node after
/// coalescing is leaked, and counted, into its pool's `dropped`; it is never
/// refused and never silently lost.
public(package) static MAX_FREE_RANGES: Int = 32

/// The same sentinel for the free-range slab.
public(package) static NO_FREE_RANGE: Int = -1

/// IoMemory objects the kernel can hold (spec §2.5).
///
/// Smaller than the RAM slab for the reason `MAX_INTERRUPTS` is small: an
/// IoMemory is a device window, and a machine has as many of those as it has
/// peripherals a driver wants. Carving is non-exclusive, so several windows
/// onto one peripheral cost several slots, so it is larger than
/// `MAX_INTERRUPTS`.
public(package) static MAX_IOMEMORIES: Int = 8

/// The same sentinel for the IoMemory slab.
public(package) static NO_IOMEMORY: Int = -1

/// Mapping objects the kernel can hold (spec §2.5).
///
/// A Mapping is one installed row with a handle on it, so the honest ceiling is
/// the rows every process's domain can hold at once. A map that meets this
/// answers `NoResource`, exactly as one that meets the hardware's row budget
/// does.
public(package) static MAX_MAPPINGS: Int = 8

/// The same sentinel for the Mapping slab.
public(package) static NO_MAPPING: Int = -1

/// Rows in a process's grant record: the protection map the scheduler replays
/// when it switches into that process.
///
/// This is only the array length. What a process may actually hold is a
/// per-profile budget the HAL publishes (`hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET`), and this
/// number is the storage that has to cover the larger of them. One profile's
/// limit is hardware (a fixed number of numbered protection regions), so a map
/// meeting it is a map the machine cannot install. The other has no such limit
/// and its limit is this policy number, chosen so one array serves both. A map
/// checks the HAL's number and never this one; the asserts below stop the array
/// being too short.
///
/// Raising it costs every process body a row of storage.
public(package) static MAX_GRANT_ROWS: Int = 9

static_assert(hal.MAX_ROOT_SEGMENTS + 1 <= 9,
              "a process's grant record must hold every segment plus its stack")

static_assert(hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET <= 9,
              "a process's grant record must hold every row its profile may install")

// Every body needs a protection domain to live in. On a tier that keeps a
// persistent table set per process, the HAL sizes a static pool and advertises
// how many sets it holds; a body with no set behind it would index past the
// pool. The number is declared in the HAL rather than read from
// here because a HAL that had to import the kernel to size its own page tables
// would be the wrong way round. This assert keeps the two spellings honest, as
// the `GRANT_ROW_BUDGET` pair above does.
//
// It binds only the compiled floor: `MAX_PROCESSES` is extent 0's length, so
// the processes the image was built for must have somewhere to live. Donation
// moves the slab's capacity past this line but cannot move the HAL's pool, so
// above the floor the same question is answered at run time by
// `kcore.process.process_slots_usable`, and a create past the pool answers the
// `NoResource` a full table already answers.
static_assert(MAX_PROCESSES <= hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS,
              "the compiled floor of process bodies must fit the HAL's domain pool")

/// Threads the kernel can hold, across all processes (compiled floor).
/// `SlabKind.Threads` raises the machine's real ceiling, and a thread donation
/// carries the frame arena with it: a donated extent holds both, at
/// `THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE` alignment, addressed through the extent table's side
/// column. See `kcore.threads.frame_slot`.
public(package) static MAX_THREADS: Int = 8

/// Events the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2.4).
public(package) static MAX_EVENTS: Int = 8

/// Waiters the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2.2). Fewer than
/// events on purpose: a Waiter is a dispatch point, and the shape §2.2 ratifies
/// (many handles per Waiter, many threads per Waiter) is one per pool of
/// workers rather than one per notification.
public(package) static MAX_WAITERS: Int = 4

/// Interrupts the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2 table, §9).
///
/// Small because an Interrupt is a bound device line, and a machine has as many
/// of those as it has devices with drivers. Each costs a masked/unmasked line
/// in the controller, a resource the board fixes.
public(package) static MAX_INTERRUPTS: Int = 4

/// Clock objects in the system (spec §2.6).
///
/// Not a slab budget but a census of domains. A hardware-backed Clock is one
/// kernel-eternal object per `ClockType`, existing from boot and owned by
/// nobody, so the slot is the domain's ordinal and nothing is allocated or
/// freed here. A virtual clock would be a separately created, stateful object
/// (offset, rate, owner) with its own creation op and lifetime; it must not
/// share this slab.
public(package) static CLOCK_KINDS: Int = 1

/// Timers the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2.6).
///
/// Sized like Events rather than Interrupts: a Timer is a per-deadline object,
/// one per timeout a process is tracking, not a per-device one.
public(package) static MAX_TIMERS: Int = 8

/// Attachments the kernel can hold, across all Waiters.
///
/// Sized to cover every waitable being attached at once: an attachment is one
/// (Waiter, waitable) pair and spec §2.2 allows an object at most one Waiter,
/// so nothing can need more than there are waitables. The terms are events,
/// interrupts, timers and process records (a Process handle is a waitable that
/// names a record, one attachment per record at most). A Clock and a thread are
/// not waitable, so `CLOCK_KINDS` and `MAX_THREADS` are absent.
///
/// The pipe waitables (outlet, inlet, claim, obligation) are deliberately not
/// terms. Sizing this to cover every one at once would multiply the table by
/// the in-flight budget. `NoResource` at `add` is what reports the wall.
///
/// Every term is itself a compiled floor, so on a machine that donates,
/// "nothing can need more attachments than there are waitables" no longer
/// holds: a deployment that donates events can have more events than this sum,
/// and `NoResource` at `Waiter.add` reports it. `Attachments` is itself a
/// donatable kind, so a deployment that raises a waitable slab raises this one
/// with the same op. The sum still guarantees the boot minimum for a machine
/// that never donates.
public(package) static MAX_ATTACHMENTS: Int = (MAX_EVENTS + MAX_INTERRUPTS
                                               + MAX_TIMERS + MAX_PROCESS_RECORDS)

/// Pipe connections the kernel can hold (spec §2.1), compiled floor.
///
/// One slab serves the pair, not one per end: a `PipeInlet` and a `PipeOutlet`
/// are two counted kinds over one connection slot, so this counts live
/// connections. A connection frees when both of its reference columns reach
/// zero.
///
/// Small because a slot is not free: it carries `PIPE_INFLIGHT` staging slots
/// of `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` each, the only per-object storage in the kernel
/// measured in kilobytes. A connection is a per-client relationship and a
/// hand-written system has a handful. A service's connection count tracks its
/// sessions, not its clients (sibling inlets are per-client authority).
///
/// `SlabKind.Pipes` raises the ceiling, and a pipe donation carries the staging
/// rings with it: one unit is a connection slot plus `PIPE_INFLIGHT` exchange
/// records, by far the most expensive kind per slot.
public(package) static MAX_PIPES: Int = 4

/// The same sentinel for the Pipe slab.
public(package) static NO_PIPE: Int = -1

/// Messages one connection may hold staged (spec §2.1).
///
/// Derived from `MAX_THREADS`: two per thread, so a system in which every
/// thread has a request in flight and a reply outstanding cannot fill the ring
/// on the sender's own account. The staging is a small per-connection ring of
/// fixed slots, which keeps spec §2.1's property of zero dynamic kernel
/// allocation while letting a client post again before the server has taken the
/// first.
///
/// It is deliberately not published to userspace: a ring's depth is a slab
/// dimension, and a caller that meets a full one is told `WouldBlock` without
/// needing the number. `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is the half that is a published
/// contract, so it lives in `sosabi`.
public(package) static PIPE_INFLIGHT: Int = 2 * MAX_THREADS

static_assert(PIPE_INFLIGHT >= 1,
              "a connection must be able to stage at least one message")
static_assert(PIPE_INFLIGHT <= 64,
              "the staging ring is inline per connection — keep the storage bounded")

/// Handles one process's table can hold (§3).
public(package) static MAX_HANDLES: Int = 16

// =============================================================================
// The extent chain
// =============================================================================
//
// Every slab budget above (each `MAX_<KIND>` except `MAX_HANDLES` and
// `MAX_GRANT_ROWS`, which are fixed array lengths) is a floor, not a ceiling:
// it is extent 0's length, what the image is built with and what a machine that
// never donates gets. `SlabDonate` raises the machine's actual capacity above
// it.

/// Donated extents one slab may accept, beyond its compiled extent 0.
///
/// The number is about the table rather than the memory: each extent costs a
/// base and a slot count in every converted slab's static, paid by kinds that
/// are never donated to as well. A deployment wanting one kind very large
/// donates one large region rather than several small ones. Donation is
/// permanent, so nothing is spent and re-spent.
///
/// A donation to a kind that already has this many extents answers
/// `NoResource`: the machine cannot do this right now.
public(package) static MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS: Int = 4

/// The largest total capacity any one slab may reach.
///
/// `HANDLE_INDEX_BITS` does not bound this. A handle word is
/// `(generation, index)` where the index names a row of the caller's own handle
/// table (spec §3), and the row names the slab slot in a full-width `Int` field
/// (`HandleEntry.target`), so a slab may grow past `1 << HANDLE_INDEX_BITS`
/// without an extra index bit. `HANDLE_INDEX_BITS` bounds `MAX_HANDLES`, the
/// per-process table (the assert lives with the decoder, in `kcore.objects`):
/// however many Events the machine can hold, one process can name at most
/// `MAX_HANDLES` of them at once. The relation pinned below is that the slab
/// ceiling never stops every process from filling its table.
public(package) static MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY: Int = 1 << 20

static_assert(MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY >= MAX_HANDLES * MAX_PROCESSES,
              "a slab must be able to back every handle every process could hold")

static_assert(MAX_SLAB_CAPACITY < Int.max / 1024,
              "slot arithmetic (index * slot bytes) must not overflow a platform Int")

static_assert(MAX_SLAB_EXTENTS >= 1,
              "a slab that can accept no donation is not a chain")

/// Bytes reserved per thread for its saved context.
///
/// A stride, not a size: the frame layout is the HAL's and differs per profile
/// (a different number of words on each), so the kernel reserves a
/// generous power-of-two slot and asserts the machine's real frame fits. The
/// stride is also the alignment the arena is rounded to, which gives each frame
/// the 16-byte alignment one profile's stack pointer requires.
public(package) static THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE: Int = 512

static_assert(hal.FRAME_BYTES <= THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE,
              "this machine's trap frame does not fit the kernel's per-thread stride")

static_assert(THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE % 16 == 0,
              "a frame slot must be 16-byte aligned on every profile")

/// One extra stride of slack, so rounding the arena's base up to the stride
/// cannot run the last slot past the end.
public(package) static FRAME_ARENA_WORDS: Int = (THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE / 8) * (MAX_THREADS + 1)

static_assert(CLOCK_KINDS == 1,
              "one slot per ClockType, and v1 declares exactly one domain")

/// "No thread". Slot indices are ordinary 0-based `Int`s in the tables and
/// plus-one inside list links; this is the sentinel for the plain form.
public(package) static NO_THREAD: Int = -1

/// The same sentinel for the other tables. There is none for clocks: every
/// domain's slot exists from boot, so "no clock" is not a state the kernel can
/// be in.
public(package) static NO_EVENT: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_WAITER: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_INTERRUPT: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_ATTACHMENT: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_TIMER: Int = -1

// =============================================================================
// The quota defaults (spec §2.9)
// =============================================================================
//
// A slab size says how many of a thing the machine can hold; a default says how
// many one child may hold of that. Every number below is smaller than the slab
// above it, so a policy cap is never above the table it sits under.
//
// These are the child's numbers only. Root is init and its policy cap is the
// machine, so root's rows are `QUOTA_UNLIMITED` and the slab is what refuses
// it; see `install_quota_limits` in `kcore.objects`. There is no per-create
// quota argument: a launcher gets these numbers for every child it makes.
//
// A thread row counts the thread slots a process holds, exited ones still named
// included: it is credited when a slot is freed, or when its thread is
// invalidated (spec §2.10's refund rule). So the row bounds a child's share of
// the thread slab, and an `Exited` slot whose handle is never released keeps
// its row until the process dies.
/// Threads a child may hold at once.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_THREADS: Int = 4

/// Events a child may hold at once (spec §2.4).
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_EVENTS: Int = 4

/// Waiters a child may hold at once. Fewer than events for the reason
/// `MAX_WAITERS` is: a Waiter is a dispatch point.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_WAITERS: Int = 2

/// IRQ lines a child may hold bound at once. Small because a driver child
/// drives a device, not a workload.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_INTERRUPTS: Int = 2

/// Timers a child may hold at once.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_TIMERS: Int = 4

/// RAM regions a child may hold at once — its own, plus what it has split.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_MEMORIES: Int = 4

/// Device windows a child may hold at once.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_IOMEMORIES: Int = 2

/// Grant rows a child's domain may carry beyond the ones its image spent.
///
/// This row counts installed rows, not Mapping objects, the one place the
/// ledger departs from "quotas count objects". See `QuotaKind.Mapping`.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_MAPPINGS: Int = 2

/// Pipe connections a child may hold at once (spec §2.1).
///
/// Creator pays: this row counts what a child made, not what it was handed. A
/// child given one end of a connection its launcher created spends a handle row
/// like any other capability, which lets a server child hold many sessions
/// under a small pipe budget. The row is small because a connection slot is the
/// most expensive object per slot (see `MAX_PIPES`).
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_PIPES: Int = 2

/// Handle-table entries a child may hold at once (spec §3).
///
/// Below `MAX_HANDLES` on purpose, so a child meets its own budget rather than
/// the table's.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_HANDLES: Int = 12

/// Grant rows a v1 image spends before its process runs: `code|rodata|rw`
/// collapses to two loadable segments, plus the kernel's stack grant at the
/// region's top. Root's image and children's images spend the same.
public(package) static IMAGE_GRANT_ROWS: Int = 3

/// The smaller of the two profiles' row budgets, written down so the assert
/// below can be compile-time. `hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET` is a `UInt` and the quota
/// numbers are `Int`s; the minimum is stated here and pinned against the HAL on
/// the next line rather than cast in a `static_assert`.
public(package) static MIN_GRANT_ROW_BUDGET: Int = 8

static_assert(hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET >= 8,
              "MIN_GRANT_ROW_BUDGET is stale — this profile grants fewer rows than it claims")

// The mapping default is chosen so that a child on it can always spend its
// whole budget without the machine refusing first, on either profile (the
// smaller is what is tested). That makes `install_row`'s `fatal` a statement
// about a kernel bug rather than a number picked too large.
//
// The run-time half is `install_quota_limits`, which clamps every process's
// mapping row to what its own domain has left after its image, something a
// compile-time test cannot know.
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_MAPPINGS + IMAGE_GRANT_ROWS <= MIN_GRANT_ROW_BUDGET,
              "a child's mapping quota must fit the smaller profile's free grant rows")

// Every default is under the slab it draws from, so a child meets its own
// budget rather than the machine's. A default at or above its slab would make
// `QuotaExceeded` unreachable for that kind.
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_THREADS < MAX_THREADS, "a thread default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_EVENTS < MAX_EVENTS, "an event default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_WAITERS < MAX_WAITERS, "a waiter default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_INTERRUPTS < MAX_INTERRUPTS, "an interrupt default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_TIMERS < MAX_TIMERS, "a timer default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_MEMORIES < MAX_MEMORIES, "a memory default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_IOMEMORIES < MAX_IOMEMORIES, "an iomemory default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_MAPPINGS < MAX_MAPPINGS, "a mapping default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_PIPES < MAX_PIPES, "a pipe default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_HANDLES < MAX_HANDLES, "a handle default must be under the table")
